<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Sturm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jacob Sturm von Sturmeck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Sturm was a statesman of the free imperial city of Strasbourg who led its government and diplomacy for three decades. He guided the city's adoption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1520s alongside the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a>, represented it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diets</a>, and was a leading figure of the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. He also helped found Strasbourg's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-sturm">Johannes Sturm</a> in 1538.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacob-sturm.jpg" alt="Jacob Sturm" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reproduction photographique Claude TRUONG-NGOC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_Sturm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacob Sturm von Sturmeck was born in Strasbourg on 10 August 1489, the son of Martin Sturm, a man of some standing in the city. He studied at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and Freiburg, and about 1517 entered the service of Henry, provost of Strasbourg, a member of the Wittelsbach family. He soon took up the reformed doctrines, left the provost's service and in 1524 joined the governing body of his native city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sturm directed Strasbourg's policy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a> and represented the city at the Diet of Speyer in 1526. At the Diet of Speyer of 1529 he signed the protest on Strasbourg's behalf, making him one of the original Protestants. Working for unity among the reformers, he attended the conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> in 1529, and when the attempt to reconcile Lutherans and Zwinglians failed, he presented the Zwinglian Confessio tetrapolitana to the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1530. He was on friendly terms with Philip, landgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p><p>Largely through his influence Strasbourg joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> in 1531. Its troops took the field when the league attacked the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1546, but in February 1547 the city had to submit, and Sturm secured very favourable terms from the emperor. He also won some modification for Strasbourg of the Interim issued at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in May 1548.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sturm founded the city's library and died in Strasbourg on 30 October 1553. It was said that he had been in the pay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but this is thought very unlikely. He is remembered as one of the leading promoters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacob Sturm von Sturmeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It adopted the Reformation in the 1520s under Martin Bucer, while <b>Jacob Sturm</b> led its diplomacy and Johannes Sturm founded its Gymnasium.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Free Imperial City of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Strasbourg, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Strasbourg, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.57,7.75&amp;year=1530" aria-label="Strasbourg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4730 466 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacob Sturm"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.57,7.75&amp;year=1530">What was here in 1530? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
